    {
      "id": 106861,
      "name": "Craig",
      "username": "Craig",
      "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/craig/{size}/2150_2.png",
      "created_at": "2014-06-21T11:40:41.000Z",
      "cooked": "<p>It's super easy to get attached to characters in the Mouse Guard RPG. Perfect example: I GMed the last game I played (ok, every game for the last 6-7 years. I like to GM), and I had an owl villain that I actually tried to convince my players not to kill in the end. I never do that, because I fully believe in player agency. But I got so attached to this owl, his motivations and life that it seemed sad to kill him. However, in the fiction, they really had no choice, so my pleading fell on deaf ears.</p><p>The only NPC I've ever been that attached to.</p><p>As far as the graphic novels go, they're good, and I highly recommend reading them. The RPG system was perfectly suited to the stories, really. Those characters all have life beyond the pages, and it's easy to fall into their shoes.</p>",
